# LF line ends in every checkout, on Windows too, where Git writes CRLF by default: a build on Windows (the Tests
# workflow's job on Windows) then reads the same files as on Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The decision logs (docs/decisions/) merge by union: Git keeps the lines of both sides, with no conflict. So union
# fits only files that grow by new entries at their end. What to read after a merge: docs/decisions/README.md. The
# driver `decision-log` is `git merge-file --union --diff3`: the diff3 form keeps the blank line before each new
# entry, which Git's built-in union drops for the second entry. The driver runs only where Git's settings define it
# (docs/working-notes.md). Without it, Git merges these files as text and shows each conflict. The log's README does
# not merge by union.
docs/decisions/*.md merge=decision-log
docs/decisions/README.md !merge
